question:
Equilateral triangle $ABC$ and square $BCDE$ are coplanar, as shown. What is the number of degrees in the measure of angle $CAD$?

[asy]
size(70);
draw((0,0)--(20,0)--(20,20)--(0,20)--cycle);
draw((0,20)--(10,37.3)--(20,20));
draw((10,37.3)--(20,0));
label("$A$",(10,37.3),N);
label("$B$",(0,20),W);
label("$C$",(20,20),E);
label("$D$",(20,0),E);
label("$E$",(0,0),W);
[/asy]

ground_truth:
First, observe that $AC=CD$.  Therefore, triangle $ACD$ is isosceles and $\angle CAD$ is congruent to $\angle CDA$.  Also, $m\angle ACD=m\angle ACB+m\angle BCD=60^\circ+90^\circ=150^\circ$.  Since the three angles of triangle $ACD$ sum to 180 degrees, we have  \begin{align*}
m\angle CAD+m\angle CDA+150^\circ&=180^\circ \implies \\
2m\angle CAD&=30^\circ\implies \\
m\angle CAD&=\boxed{15} \text{ degrees}.
\end{align*}

question:
In the diagram, $QRS$ is a straight line. What is the measure of $\angle RPS,$ in degrees? [asy]
pair Q=(0,0);
pair R=(1.3,0);
pair SS=(2.3,0);
pair P=(.8,1);

draw(P--Q--R--SS--P--R);

label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$R$",R,S);
label("$S$",SS,S);
label("$P$",P,N);

label("$48^\circ$",Q+(.12,.05),NE);
label("$67^\circ$",P-(.02,.15),S);
label("$38^\circ$",SS+(-.32,.05),NW);

[/asy]

ground_truth:
Since the sum of the angles in a triangle is $180^\circ,$ then \begin{align*}
\angle QPS &= 180^\circ - \angle PQS - \angle PSQ \\
&= 180^\circ - 48^\circ - 38^\circ \\
&= 94^\circ.
\end{align*}Therefore, \begin{align*}
\angle RPS &= \angle QPS - \angle QPR \\
&= 94^\circ - 67^\circ \\
&= \boxed{27^\circ}.
\end{align*}

